["er.record/1",{"commit":{"envelope":{"actor":"human:timo","causation":"cmd-child-75c977675c45be8eb0c5fe11adf56f5a145c661b4f903aabb7fdf7745d0a3980","correlation":"protocol-artifact-new","record":{"changed":{"document":{"events":[],"fields":{"intent":{"causation":null,"command_id":"cmd-child-75c977675c45be8eb0c5fe11adf56f5a145c661b4f903aabb7fdf7745d0a3980","command_type":"aep.entity.create/v1","correlation_id":"protocol-artifact-new","execution_id":null,"expected_revision":null,"payload":{"command":"create-entity","data":{"body":"## Context\n\nAn agent writes through `ekr propose`, `ekr validate` and `ekr commit` and takes ids from `ekr mint`, `ekr ontology` and `ekr snapshot` (AGENTS.md, \"Agents that use the ekr binary\"). Today its only way to find an existing node is to read `ekr snapshot` and match by eye or by name, which is the name matching the resolver replaces. This story puts `story:typed-reference-resolver` behind a read verb.\n\n- `ekr resolve <reference.yaml> [--at <revision>]` reads a typed-reference document and prints the `ResolutionOutcome` as one JSON document (exit 0), or a named refusal (exit 2, its name on stderr). It opens only an existing store and writes nothing.\n- `ekr schema` prints the typed-reference JSON Schema; `ekr example typed-reference` prints one.\n- `ekr guide` tells an agent to resolve before `CreateNode`, and that `ProposeNew` means \"mint an id and create\", not \"retry with a looser reference\".\n- AGENTS.md requires `docs/cli.md` to change with the verb; `crates/ekr/tests/docs_cli.rs` runs the worked example on both providers.\n\n## Design sections\n\n§ 45, § 63 step 3, § 70; A10.\n\n## Domain relations\n\n- `Node → NodeType`, many-to-one, a reference. Inferable (`systems/ekr/domains/graph.yaml`, `ekr.graph.Node` relation `type`, via `type_id`). The verb adds no relation of its own; the rest are those of `story:typed-reference-resolver`.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `crates/ekr/src/cli/` (a new `resolve.rs`, plus `mod.rs` and `agent.rs` for the guide and example)\n- `crates/ekr/Cargo.toml`: the dependency on `ekr-integrate`\n- `docs/cli.md`, `crates/ekr/tests/docs_cli.rs`, `crates/ekr/tests/agent_cli.rs`\n\n## Acceptance\n\n`ekr resolve` given a typed-reference document prints its resolution as one JSON document and leaves the store byte-identical, and the `docs/cli.md` worked example that runs it passes on both providers.\n","status":"draft","title":"ekr resolve: an agent resolves a typed reference before it proposes a node"},"entity_type":"aep.story/v1","locator":"ep://planning/store/story/ekr-resolve-verb"},"target":null,"task":null},"result":{"affected":["ent-00102573ce5bace5db21ee522b7193da@1"],"audit":["aud-fd42ec7c1b12b2a073c4026456a0418f"],"command_id":"cmd-child-75c977675c45be8eb0c5fe11adf56f5a145c661b4f903aabb7fdf7745d0a3980","consistency":"seq-000000000000","events":["evt-fd42ef7c1b12b7b973c4036456a04342"],"outcome":"accepted"}},"lifecycle_state":"recorded"}},"command":{"command":"create","fields":{"document":{"events":[],"fields":{"intent":{"causation":null,"command_id":"cmd-child-75c977675c45be8eb0c5fe11adf56f5a145c661b4f903aabb7fdf7745d0a3980","command_type":"aep.entity.create/v1","correlation_id":"protocol-artifact-new","execution_id":null,"expected_revision":null,"payload":{"command":"create-entity","data":{"body":"## Context\n\nAn agent writes through `ekr propose`, `ekr validate` and `ekr commit` and takes ids from `ekr mint`, `ekr ontology` and `ekr snapshot` (AGENTS.md, \"Agents that use the ekr binary\"). Today its only way to find an existing node is to read `ekr snapshot` and match by eye or by name, which is the name matching the resolver replaces. This story puts `story:typed-reference-resolver` behind a read verb.\n\n- `ekr resolve <reference.yaml> [--at <revision>]` reads a typed-reference document and prints the `ResolutionOutcome` as one JSON document (exit 0), or a named refusal (exit 2, its name on stderr). It opens only an existing store and writes nothing.\n- `ekr 